WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.262
A LiDAR spun hard up a narrow staircase.

00:00:03.262 --> 00:00:06.777
Few walls, fast turns, the case that breaks LiDAR odometry.

00:00:07.157 --> 00:00:08.981
This is PV-LIO holding on.

00:00:08.981 --> 00:00:13.360
What it changes from FAST-LIO2 is how much it trusts each point.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:16.871
Hi, I'm Plank!

00:00:16.871 --> 00:00:20.212
Here is how PV-LIO weighs every LiDAR point, and what happened

00:00:20.212 --> 00:00:21.426
when I ran it.

00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:24.357
Here is the formula.

00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:29.016
Each return carries the sensor's noise: a centimetre along the beam, a

00:00:29.016 --> 00:00:30.908
tenth of a degree across it.

00:00:31.328 --> 00:00:34.146
Each plane it hits is a fit to noisy points, so it

00:00:34.146 --> 00:00:35.321
carries an error bar too.

00:00:35.741 --> 00:00:38.850
Their sum sets how hard that residual pulls.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.571
FAST-LIO2 gives every residual the same weight.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.475
A return is a range and a direction.

00:00:45.475 --> 00:00:49.579
Range noise stretches it along the beam; bearing noise widens it with distance.

00:00:49.959 --> 00:00:53.562
Then the pose's own uncertainty is added, to put the point in the world.

00:00:54.992 --> 00:00:58.366
The map is a hash of root voxels, and every voxel tries

00:00:58.366 --> 00:00:59.490
to hold one plane.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:03.095
When a voxel's points are flat enough, it fits a plane and

00:01:03.095 --> 00:01:04.687
stores its six by six covariance.

00:01:05.107 --> 00:01:07.985
When they are not, it is cut into eight, and each child

00:01:07.985 --> 00:01:09.664
tries again, down to the last layer.

00:01:10.307 --> 00:01:13.985
A new point matches a plane only if its distance is

00:01:13.985 --> 00:01:15.322
inside three standard deviations.

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:20.152
Then a near, head-on return pulls hard, and a far, grazing one barely counts.

00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:25.599
On a city drive the planes come out large and certain on

00:01:25.599 --> 00:01:28.363
roads and walls, small and doubtful on cars and trees.

00:01:29.658 --> 00:01:32.799
The README's own side by side, in a Hilti stairwell.

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:34.370
That is the author's run.

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.568
So I ran it myself, without ROS.

00:01:37.983 --> 00:01:39.865
The C++ compiles as written.

00:01:41.128 --> 00:01:44.084
Every ROS header it includes becomes a plain struct.

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:47.585
A feeder hands its own main loop one message at a

00:01:47.585 --> 00:01:48.705
time, in timestamp order.

00:01:49.125 --> 00:01:53.031
Then the shootout harness scores the poses against each dataset's reference.

00:01:54.592 --> 00:01:58.716
On the Oxford walk the Hilti config is the worst of three.

00:01:58.716 --> 00:02:02.153
Swap its weights for FAST-LIO2's constant and the error halves.

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:05.301
And the map never forgets.

00:02:05.301 --> 00:02:09.703
Quarter-metre voxels over a whole quad, kept forever, peaked near nine gigabytes.

00:02:10.942 --> 00:02:14.753
With one constant weight, the basement ties and the quad improves.

00:02:14.753 --> 00:02:16.139
But the stairwells diverge.

00:02:16.559 --> 00:02:20.587
Weighted, as shipped, it is the only run that holds together in the stairwells.

00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:25.420
Weighing each point by its uncertainty is the right idea.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.305
But the noise model is two hand-set numbers, and their scale decides

00:02:29.305 --> 00:02:31.571
how far the LiDAR outweighs the IMU.

00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:36.615
Great indoors, pick the other config outdoors, and measure your noise.

00:02:36.615 --> 00:02:37.583
Thanks for watching!

00:02:38.003 --> 00:02:40.062
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:40.062 --> 00:02:40.651
I'm Plank.

00:02:40.651 --> 00:02:40.945
Bye!
